{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfvX6sy4WcfM5SMwumB3y","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I ruled finding DA as **FIX**. The verdict is in `docs/reviews/wi-902/004-ADJUDICATE-d8779c8.md`, committed as `c2c09cf6b` with the `WI: WI-902` trailer. The commit hooks passed.\n\n**What's wrong:** in the lane, `_standing_successors` (`project-trajectory/scripts/intake.py:2557`) treats an exact title match as proof that the row was minted earlier. It then takes that row's own `Supersedes` list as the successor set. So a disposition of one row can silently rewrite the dependencies of a different, unrelated row.\n\n**Reproduction:** I ran the builder's fixture through the real `intake_after_merge` myself, twice:\n- **At the lane's last commit (`69c123798`):** WI-041 used to need WI-009. It now needs only WI-040, and WI-042, the other successor of WI-009, is gone. The run still reports success.\n- **Against the pre-range `intake.py` (`1ff05bc06`, loaded in memory):** WI-041 still needs WI-009, untouched.\n\nSo this range introduced the regression. With WI-040 done and WI-042 still queued, the scheduler would let WI-041 start too early.\n\n**Scope:** to reproduce it you only need titles and `Supersedes` entries that agents write into the repo, so it falls inside the PROCESS.md §6 threat model. The fix is small and sits where the mint happens: check that the title match agrees with the draft's intended `supersedes`, and refuse a mismatch or ambiguous match before writing anything. That costs far less than the defect.\n\nThe builder also held FIX. I reached the ruling from my own runs, not from that agreement.\n\nOne related problem is noted in the verdict but not ruled, because it predates this range: when a draft's title collides with an existing row, the draft is silently dropped and never minted. The proposed refusal would also close that. If the lane doesn't fix it, it should be filed separately.\n\nI did